name: egress-vps/trusted-networks
description: "Never ban the tailnet or the house"
#
# THE RECOVERY PATH MUST NOT BE BANNABLE. Written by bootstrap.sh from
# CROWDSEC_WHITELIST — edit the variable, not this file.
#
# Two networks, for two different reasons:
#
#   100.64.0.0/10   the tailnet. This is how you reach the box when something
#                   here is wrong, and Tailscale SSH is the only login path that
#                   does not go through the public sshd. A CrowdSec decision
#                   that covered it could lock you out of the machine you need
#                   in order to remove the decision — the classic way a
#                   detection tool becomes an outage.
#
#   192.168.4.0/24  the house / cluster LAN. The cluster talks to this box as an
#                   authenticated proxy client, at volume. A false positive here
#                   does not lock out a person, it takes every opted-in
#                   service's egress down at once.
#
# A whitelist is a PARSER stage, not a scenario filter: it drops the event
# before any scenario counts it, so a whitelisted source cannot accumulate
# toward a ban at all. That is the behaviour we want — "ban them but ignore it"
# would still fill the decisions table with noise.
whitelist:
  reason: "tailnet and home LAN — the recovery path and the cluster itself"
  cidr:
@CROWDSEC_WHITELIST_CIDRS@
